---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DebtToIncomeRatioCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/DebtToIncomeRatioCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="debt-to-income-ratio-calculator">
  <DebtToIncomeRatioCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the debt-to-income ratio calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Your debt-to-income ratio (DTI) is <code>monthly debt payments ÷ gross monthly income × 100</code>. The
      calculator also shows the housing ratio, which counts only the rent or mortgage payment, and how much more
      monthly debt fits under 36% and 43%.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      With a gross income of 6,500 a month, a 1,800 housing payment, a 450 car loan, 300 of student loans and 120 of
      card minimums, debt payments are 2,670. The DTI is 2,670 ÷ 6,500 = 41.1% and the housing ratio is 27.7%.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Lenders set their own limits: 36% or less is the usual target, 43% is a common limit for a qualified US
      mortgage, and some government-backed loans go to about 50%. They also look at your credit, savings and down
      payment. Use income before tax, and count only required payments.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
